<% content_for :title, "How the three tokenizers differ · Rails Active Search on SQLite FTS5" %>
<article class="explainer">
  <header class="explainer-header">
    <h1>How the three tokenizers differ</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The <%= link_to "search page", root_path %> runs one query against three indexes of the same text and gets
      three different answers. Nothing in the query or the text changes between them. What changes is how FTS5
      cuts the text into tokens before it stores or looks anything up, and that one choice decides what a search
      can find.
    </p>
    <nav class="explainer-nav" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#tokens">Tokens</a>
      <a href="#cuts">One sentence, three cuts</a>
      <a href="#the-three">The three tokenizers</a>
      <a href="#hits">Same query, different hits</a>
      <a href="#playground">Playground</a>
      <a href="#pitfalls">Pitfalls</a>
      <a href="#choosing">Which one</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <section id="tokens" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>Search matches tokens, not text</h2>
    <p>
      A full-text index never compares your query with the text. When a section is indexed, a <strong>tokenizer</strong>
      cuts its text into tokens, and the index remembers which tokens each section contains. When you search, the
      <em>same</em> tokenizer cuts your query into tokens, and a section is a hit when it contains them.
    </p>
    <%= render "pipeline" %>
    <p>
      So <code>callbacks</code> finds the sections that contain the token <code>callbacks</code>, and nothing
      else: not <code>callback</code>, not <code>Callbacks</code> spelled differently by the tokenizer, not the
      <code>callba</code> you had typed before you hit enter. Every difference between the three columns of the
      search page comes down to which tokens each tokenizer makes of the same characters.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cuts" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>One sentence, three ways to cut it</h2>
    <p>
      Here is one sentence as each tokenizer sees it. Each chip is one token, drawn under the characters it was
      cut from.
    </p>
    <%= render "cuts", cuts: @cuts %>
    <ul class="explainer-notes">
      <li><strong>unicode61</strong> cuts at spaces and punctuation, lowercases, and keeps the words as they are. The underscore is punctuation, so <code>has_many</code> is two tokens.</li>
      <li><strong>porter</strong> starts from the same words and trims each one to a stem: <code>validations</code> loses <code>ations</code> and so would <code>validate</code> and <code>valid</code>, which is how one query reaches all three. A dashed chip is a stem that is not simply a shorter word: <code>many</code> becomes <code>mani</code>, so that <code>many</code> and <code>mania</code> meet.</li>
      <li><strong>trigram</strong> ignores words altogether: every window of three characters is a token, spaces (<code>␣</code>) and full stops included, and the windows overlap. A query of three or more characters matches wherever those characters sit, inside a word or across two.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="the-three" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>The three tokenizers</h2>
    <p>
      All three indexes are declared the same way in <code>config/search.rb</code> and written by the same
      generator. The only difference is one option on the <code>create_virtual_table</code> line of their migrations.
    </p>
    <%= render "strategies" %>
  </section>

  <section id="hits" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>Same query, different hits</h2>
    <p>
      The search page's sample queries, counted over the <%= number_with_delimiter Section.count %> sections of the
      guides as this page loads. The dark part of a bar is the sections all three tokenizers find; the light part is
      what this one finds and at least one of the others does not. Click a query to see the hits.
    </p>
    <%= render "comparison", comparisons: @comparisons %>
  </section>

  <section id="playground" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>Playground</h2>
    <p>
      Type a piece of text and a query, and see what each tokenizer makes of them and whether the text would be a
      hit. The match is FTS5's own answer, through a temporary table built with each tokenizer, and the query is
      passed the way Active Search passes it.
    </p>
    <%= render "playground_form", playground: @playground %>
    <%= render "playground_results", playground: @playground %>
  </section>

  <section id="pitfalls" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <dl class="pitfalls">
      <dt><code>has_many</code> is the phrase "has many"</dt>
      <dd>
        Active Search has no query syntax of its own: it passes a plain word to FTS5 as it is and quotes anything
        else as a phrase. <code>has_many</code> is a plain word to Active Search, but unicode61 and porter then cut it
        into <code>has</code> and <code>many</code>, which FTS5 reads as the phrase "has many". That finds every
        <code>has_many</code>, and also prose that says a model has many of something. Only trigram keeps the
        underscore. <%= link_to "See it", root_path(q: "has_many") %>.
      </dd>
      <dt><code>N+1</code> is the phrase "n 1", or one token</dt>
      <dd>
        Not a plain word, so Active Search quotes it. unicode61 and porter cut the phrase into <code>n</code> and
        <code>1</code>; trigram makes the single token <code>n+1</code>, which only the exact spelling contains.
        <%= link_to "See it", root_path(q: "N+1") %>.
      </dd>
      <dt>porter folds words that only look alike</dt>
      <dd>
        A stemmer knows spelling, not meaning. <code>general</code>, <code>generate</code>, <code>generation</code> and
        <code>generic</code> are all <code>gener</code>; <code>organ</code> and <code>organization</code> are both
        <code>organ</code>; <code>relative</code> is <code>rel</code>. A search for one finds the others, and ranks
        them as if they were the same word. Try them in the <a href="#playground">playground</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>trigram needs three characters, and matches a lot with few</dt>
      <dd>
        A query of one or two characters is no token at all, so it matches nothing. Three or four characters
        match inside many unrelated words (<code>ate</code> is in create, update, validate and template). And
        FTS5 measures a snippet in tokens, so a trigram snippet is at most 64 characters long whatever you ask for.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="choosing" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>Which one</h2>
    <table class="choosing">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>You want</th><th>Pick</th><th>Because</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>exact words, identifiers kept apart from prose, the smallest index</td>
          <td><a href="#unicode61" class="strategy-link strategy-unicode61">unicode61</a></td>
          <td>what you type is what is matched; nothing surprising, and nothing forgiving</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>English prose where plural, tense and -tion should not matter</td>
          <td><a href="#porter" class="strategy-link strategy-porter">porter</a></td>
          <td>one query reaches every form of a word, at the cost of a few false cousins</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>partial words as you type, code and identifiers, or text with no spaces between words</td>
          <td><a href="#trigram" class="strategy-link strategy-trigram">trigram</a></td>
          <td>matches by substring, which is the only way in when words are not where a tokenizer looks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Two of them can live side by side, as they do here: the index is a table, and a model may have more than one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="other-languages" class="explainer-section">
    <h2>Text without spaces</h2>
    <p>
      unicode61 and porter find words by looking for the spaces and punctuation between them. A Japanese sentence
      has neither, so the whole sentence becomes one token, and a query matches only the whole sentence. trigram
      does not look for words, so it works on such text as it does on English:
      <% japanese = TokenizerPlayground::PRESETS.last %>
      <%= link_to "try a Japanese sentence in the playground", tokenizers_path(text: japanese[:text], q: japanese[:query], anchor: "playground") %>.
      The guides are English, so the search page cannot show this, but the playground can.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
